      "pins": ["U2.2", "U2.3", "Q3.3", "J1.13", "J8.13", "R4.1"],
      "nets": ["NET0001"],
      "observed": "On the schematic, the MAX3485 DE/RE enable net is labelled NET0001, and the same NET0001 label also appears on M5Stack bus pin 13 of J1 and J8. Q3's collector and the 20 k pull-down R4 are connected to this same NET0001 node.",
      "expected": "The DE/RE enable line should be a separate control net driven by the auto-direction transistor from /TXD2, not merged with an M5Stack bus GPIO/UART pin. The specification describes Q2 as toggling DE/RE from /TXD2 and lists the bus pass-through signals without assigning pin 13 to the transceiver enable.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J1 pin 13 NET0001; J8 pin 13 NET0001; U2 pins 2/3 tied to NET0001; Q3 collector to NET0001; R4 20k from NET0001 to GND (sheet 1/1)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Q2 BC857 (PNP) Auto TX/RX direction-control transistor: driven from /TXD2 (via R1 10k, R2 5.1k) to toggle the MAX3485 DE/RE enable pins."}
      ],
      "impact": "The M5Stack bus pin 13 (G1/TXD0 per the pinout table) is shorted to the RS-485 transceiver direction-control net. This loads or fights the bus pin, can corrupt UART0/GPIO13 use, and may prevent reliable serial-servo direction control or damage the port.",
      "confidence": 0.58
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic otherwise appears to implement the specified PWM/serial jumper-selectable design, but the NET0001 label bridges the MAX3485 DE/RE enable net to M5Stack bus pin 13, which is not intended and would change board behavior."
}

